# benches/queries.paraphrase.jsonl -- SEMANTIC-ONLY paraphrase evaluation set (25 cases).
# Purpose: measure whether a real semantic embedding retriever beats a lexical/BM25 one.
# Every query is a hand-written PARAPHRASE of its target doc's topic: it would let a human or
# an embedding model pick the right document, while sharing few or no content words with it, so
# BM25 scores poorly by construction. Complement to queries.example.jsonl, whose queries reuse
# the corpus's own vocabulary and are therefore lexical by design.
# Labels are HAND-JUDGED by reading the corpus HTML (not from filenames or routing).
# Schema: search_eval::QueryCase -> {"id","query","class","relevant","graded","observed_at"}.
# classes used: papers | long_semantic | community | docs | github.
# `graded` is used only to mark a near-neighbour that is related but NOT the target (gain 1);
# where two docs are genuinely both relevant (p_bm25) both are listed in `relevant` and graded [].
# Each case is preceded by a comment naming its target doc id(s) and why it is a paraphrase.


# target arxiv/attention -- paraphrase of 'attention that replaces recurrence with parallel weighting'; the query contains none of the doc's terms (transformer, self-attention, encoder/decoder, positional encodings) and speaks of 'influencing words' instead of attention.
{"id": "para_attention", "query": "how a system can take in a whole row of words and let each one influence every other one simultaneously, without stepping through them one at a time", "class": "papers", "relevant": ["arxiv/attention"], "graded": [], "observed_at": null}
# target arxiv/chain-of-thought -- 'show its work before answering' paraphrases intermediate reasoning steps; no shared content words (chain-of-thought, prompting, reasoning, exemplars).
{"id": "para_cot", "query": "getting a model to show its work in writing before it commits to an answer noticeably helps with sums and puzzles", "class": "papers", "relevant": ["arxiv/chain-of-thought"], "graded": [], "observed_at": null}
